---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO_URL, UPSTREAM_URL, ISSUES_URL } from "../lib/releases";
---

<Base
  title="About — SparkyBin Browser"
  description="What SparkyBin Browser is, where it came from, and what it does differently from GNOME Web."
>
  <section class="border-b border-rule">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 pt-16 pb-14 sm:px-8 sm:pt-20">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-semibold sm:text-5xl">A browser, built well</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg text-ink-soft">
        SparkyBin Browser is a maintained build of GNOME Web: the same engine
        and the same interface, with the extension support reworked and the
        rough edges ironed out.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-14 sm:px-8 sm:py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-12 text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-ink">What it is</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          A web browser for the desktop. It is written in C against GTK 4 and
          libadwaita, and it renders pages with WebKitGTK, the same engine
          that Safari and most other GTK browsers use. It has tabs, a history,
          bookmarks, a private mode, a password manager, a reader mode, a
          built-in ad blocker, web apps, and Firefox Sync.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          There is no account to make, no telemetry, and nothing running in
          the background that you did not ask for.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Where it comes from</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          GNOME Web is a good browser and a well-run project, and this one is
          built on it. Upstream, WebExtension support was experimental: it was
          hidden behind a setting, and the code behind it had a number of
          problems that turned up as soon as an extension was actually
          installed and used.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The work here is on the parts that needed it. Where a fix belongs
          upstream, it is sent upstream; the changes made here are recorded in
          the
          <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/NOTICE`}>NOTICE</a>
          file, as the GPL asks, and the original copyright notices are still
          in the source.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          If none of the differences below matter to you,
          <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={UPSTREAM_URL}>GNOME Web</a>
          is worth using.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-ink">What we've changed</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          WebExtensions are on by default, and the Extensions page has a
          switch to turn them off again. The extension implementation was
          read through and fixed. The most serious things found were a
          JavaScript injection in <code class="font-mono text-sm">downloads.search()</code>,
          where a filename with a quote in it could run code in the user
          interface process, a path traversal in
          <code class="font-mono text-sm">downloads.download()</code>, where a
          filename of <code class="font-mono text-sm">../../foo</code> could
          be written outside the Downloads directory, and the extension API
          being installed in cross-origin frames, which would have handed the
          extension's identity to the page embedding it.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The application ID, the executable name and the profile directory
          changed too, so that a SparkyBin Browser installation and a GNOME
          Web installation on the same machine do not share history, cookies,
          bookmarks or extensions.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The extension work did not stop at the first release. A set of well
          known extensions was installed and run to see how far each one got,
          and four of them took the whole browser down. Five faults were
          found and fixed, and the calls that extensions were asking for and
          not getting are there now: whether the extension has a permission,
          the events for the browser starting and for the extension being
          installed, the title and the icon of a button, and the events that
          say what a tab is doing.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          An ad blocker can be used now. WebKit has no way to let an
          extension stop a request by itself, so the browser compiles the
          filter lists that come with an extension into the rules that
          WebKit's content blockers understand, and puts them on the pages.
          It is the arrangement Safari uses, and it is what makes the whole
          of uBlock Origin's list of filters work here.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The sidebar shows either the bookmarks or the open tabs, with a
          switch at the top of it, and the groups of bookmarks are the user's
          to choose and order. There are fifteen colour palettes to pick
          from, named after the projects they come from, and the browser
          keeps its own sizes so that a theme cannot squash it.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          The extension code is not finished. Manifest V3 is not supported,
          <code class="font-mono text-sm">storage.onChanged</code> is missing,
          and the architecture write-up in the repository says what is known
          to be broken.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Where it is going</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          0.2 is the second release. It is a working browser, not a finished
          product: an extension that uses a service worker does nothing yet,
          because Manifest V3 is not supported. The plan for what comes next
          is in
          <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/PLAN.md`}>PLAN.md</a>
          in the repository.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          If something is missing or broken, the
          <a class="text-accent hover:underline" href={ISSUES_URL}>issue tracker</a>
          is the place to say so.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
